 the existing reference-route state join; planar relation to coarse masked OSW display polygons.","state_semantic_exclusions":[]}],"measurement_ids":["measurement:0017"],"nasa_links":1,"object_url":"object.html?id=current%3Aeast-greenland-coastal","published_length_km":null,"recorded_claims":57,"route_decision_ids":["route-decision:east-greenland-coastal"],"route_ids":["east-greenland-coastal-reach-reference-path-candidate"],"route_url":"reference-routes.html#east-greenland-coastal-reach-reference-path-candidate","scope_notes":[{"audit_file":"research/east-greenland-coastal-reach-scope-audit.json","current_id":"east-greenland-coastal","label":"Coastal studied reach, northern uncertainty and local threshold width","observed_period":null,"source_locator":"Sutherland and Pickart (2008), section 3: Cape Farewell and 66 N section geography; section 3.5: weak assumed northern jet near 68 N; sections 8-9: merging near 63 N and approximately 1000 km shelf survey description. Relevant text inspected, no current-axis figure digitized.","source_url":"https://www.whoi.edu/cms/files/Sutherland_EGCC_revised_withfigs_33743.pdf","summary":"The 66 N to Cape Farewell editorial reach is separate from the source approximately 1000 km shelf survey. Weak northern identification and merging near 63 N limit continuity claims. The 30 km Cape Farewell width uses a 15% inner-jet velocity threshold and cannot buffer this whole route or supply annual margins."}],"season_url":"seasons.html?current=east-greenland-coastal","section_fingerprints":{"claims":"c12630f0902d24b11d2b68285e4dfde7f54d57c0ecb63f41da0e38a041b851b0","identity":"6fadc1ff71688da4cfc774943c4c194bb53553628b23d6d75c10afa261fb07f1","measurements":"6ec3a1a467f5fe4da5182020988b557776e6f4c45307e644b79f4ca00d09e1f1","media":"4e592905ba53ddd84c058bb3a8f233ac831f46c4d2862f758c3a7860773777bd","routes_geometry":"c74c9d9eda9a317996f8edfbd7adef50f67ef192e1587d4d1c4f20adf1b357f3","sources":"74744be2142746e22502fe5050c336859e2b33208a3053a7b2f8