ilation years are not route observation dates.","Resolve western formation and eastern continuation; transport bands and PV-front spans must remain separate from current width."],"reported_ensemble_angular_width":{"annual_range_supported":false,"boundary_method":"Neutral-layer zonal-velocity sign transition where available; subjective local minimum when adjacent eastward currents merge. Paired edges underlying the general 2-degree statement are not extracted.","branch_assignment":"main SSCC; secondary branch width unextracted","core_position_variation_is_width_range":false,"exact_section_geometry":null,"fixed_depth_bounds_m":null,"independent_jet_measurements":false,"latitude_span_degrees":2,"observed_period":null,"pv_front_width_is_current_width":false,"sampling_note":"Stream-coordinate averaging; spatial bins have uneven time coverage and depth-dependent sampling.","scope":"General meridional SCC width in discussion of stream-coordinate averages; not a separate regional value for each jet.","shared_source_claim":true,"source_claim_id":"rowe-2000-scc-general-two-degree-width","source_locator":"Section 3b, printed 1179 / PDF index 7; boundary methodology section 2, printed 1175.","source_pdf_url":"https://floats.pmel.noaa.gov/sites/default/files/atoms/files/gcj_1y.pdf","source_url":"https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/phoc/30/6/1520-0485_2000_030_1172_pescvt_2.0.co_2.xml"},"route_construction_status":"editorial_historical_mean_core_studied_reach_constructed","schema":"osw.current-source-scope-audit.v1","scope":"Declared historical southern mean-core studied reach from 155 W 3.5 S to 110 W 5.5 S. These gates truncate the larger southern subsurface flow. Western formation, secondary-jet geometry and eastern continuation are excluded. This is one study-defined connection, not an aggregate centerline or sum of primary and secondary branches.","source_access":"Johnson and Moore sections 2-3/text Table 1 read; Rowe et al. introduction, data-method passages and section 3e/Fig. 10 caption read from primary NOAA-hosted PDF. Figures not digitized; no full-paper read or field extraction claimed. Added inspection of Rowe et al. section 2 sampling/boundary methods and section 3b general width statement in NOAA PDF, confirmed in publisher full text; no figures or paired edges digitized.","source_branch_topology_status":"independent_origin_versus_split_unresolved","source_citation":"Johnson and Moore (1997), The Pacific Subsurface Countercurrents and an Inertial Model, Journal of Physical Oceanography 27, 2448-2459.","source_core_depths_are_uniform_route_layer":false,"source_core_support":[{"latitude":-3.5,"local_peak_depth_m":250,"longitude":-155},{"latitude":-5.5,"local_peak_depth_m":160,"longitude":-110}],"source_ensemble_period_is_route_date":false,"source_ensemble_years":[1967,1996],"source_front_scale":{"approximate_span_km":40,"bound_kind":"likely_upper_bound_due_to_density_resolution","is_annual_width_range":false,"is_whole_current_width":false,"source_locator":"Rowe et al. (2000) pp. 1174-1175, methods: stream-coordinate averaging, density resolution and front-scale limitation.","variable":"potential_vorticity_front"},"source_locator":"Sections 2-3 and Table 1: south SSCC peaks at 155 W 3.5 S/250 m and 110 W 5.5 S/160 m. Latitude integration bands are not widths. Rowe et al. (2000), introduction and section 3e/Fig. 10 caption, distinguish primary and secondary southern jets.","source_named_branches":[{"source":"Rowe et al. (2000)","source_label":"primary SSCC / main SSCC","support":"Fig. 10 caption describes a primary core around 4 S at 155 W; not assigned automatically to historical mean-core axis."},{"source":"Rowe et al. (2000)","source_label":"secondary SSCC","support":"Fig. 10 caption describes a secondary core around 6.5 S at 155 W; section 3e retains uncertainty about independent origin versus splitting."}],"source_transport_integration_bounds":[{"latitude_range":[-8,-3],"longitude":-155},{"latitude_range":[-6,-3],"longitude":-110}],"source_url":"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john1806/john1806.shtml","status":"editorial_source_scope_audit_not_canonical","transport_integration_bounds_are_current_width":false,"whole_current_length_km":null,"whole_current_width_km":null},"note":{"audit_file":"research/pacific-sscc-mean-reach-branch-width-scope-audit.json","current_id":"pacific-south-subsurface-countercurrent","label":"Historical southern mean cores versus primary/secondary jets","observed_period":null,"source_locator":"Sections 2-3 and Table 1: south SSCC peaks at 155 W 3.5 S/250 m and 110 W 5.5 S/160 m. Latitude integration bands are not widths. Rowe et al. (2000), introduction and section 3e/Fig. 10 caption, distinguish primary and secondary southern jets.","source_url":"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john1806/john1806.shtml","summary":"The 155 W to 110 W historical southern mean-core reach is one editorial connection, not a combined primary/secondary axis. Rowe et al. distinguish primary SSCC and secondary SSCC but leave independent origin versus splitting unresolved. Local 250 m/160 m mean cores do not define a uniform layer; transport integration bands and a roughly 40 km PV front are not current width or seasonal ranges. A separate Rowe et al. general 2-degree meridional width summary is now retained as approximately 220 km; the two jet records share one source claim, not independent observations or annual extrema."},"source_sha256":"8b7bb77b97b52fb8bca50aab8ff886f8302d3ff792175edf8494a1bc65d41ec2"}],"source_catalog_file":"research/ocean-current-reference-path-candidates.json","source_catalog_sha256":"b06caed27655c39f8127ddda441cba0a856800f599ec52edcd5b30d2ad37b633","source_decision":{"candidate_ids":["pacific-sscc-mean-reach-reference-path-candidate"],"component_inventory_status":"not_assessed_by_this_planning_crosswalk","current_id":"pacific-south-subsurface-countercurrent","existing_length_evidence_status":"no_numeric_length","existing_scope":"The Pacific