 Atlantic Current","name_source_url":"https://www.tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/glossary.html","next_action":"Resolve source naming and branch extent before route construction; no transfer of neighboring current dimensions. See research/spitsbergen-atlantic-naming-branch-scope-audit.json.","planning_basis":"No comparable path-length estimate admitted yet.","planning_status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","published_length_rank_eligible":false,"reference_path_decision":"reference_path_not_constructed","route_strategy_id":"resolve_naming_scope","route_strategy_label":"Resolve naming or extent convention","strategy_next_action":"Read competing definitions, resolve aliases versus distinct flows, and retain source-convention routes without merging their extents."},"source_url":"https://www.tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/glossary.html","status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","strategy_id":"resolve_naming_scope","strategy_label":"Resolve naming or extent convention"},{"candidate_count":1,"construction_status":"editorial_candidate_present_scientific_review_pending","current_id":"tasman-front","entity_id":"current:tasman-front","id":"route-decision:tasman-front","label":"Tasman Front","next_action":"Review the existing editorial route against scientific gates; broader/system/family scope remains governed by the planning strategy.","rank_eligible":false,"route_ids":["tasman-historical-connection-reach-reference-path-candidate"],"scope":"The East Australian Current separates eastward as the Tasman Front at a variable latitude, reported between 28°S and 37°S. The cited study does not give stable whole-front endpoints or an along-flow length; do not add this branch to the East Australian Current's coastal floor. A 2019 review found the front rarely distinct in instantaneous fields; a single stable path length would overstate that evidence.","scope_reviews":[{"document":{"annual_length_range_km":null,"annual_width_range_km":null,"as_of":"2026-10-03","continuity_assessment":{"annual_geometry_playback_eligible":false,"broad_weak_central_longitude_band_east":[158,168],"coherent_route_constructed":false,"continuous_front_absent_at_all_times":false,"eddy_field_implies_no_eastward_transport":false,"front_temperature_gradient_implies_current_width":false,"interpretation":"Time averaging can smear changing pathways; a persistent narrow coherent jet cannot be assumed from the mean. The reassessment also considers discontinuous streams and eddies.","mean_flow_implies_instantaneous_axis":false,"source_convention":"Oke et al. (2019), indexed Introduction: CARS 2009 steric-height climatology, relative to 2000 m."},"current_id":"tasman-front","historical_composite":{"citation":"Tilburg, Hurlburt, O’Brien and Shriver (2001), The Dynamics of the East Australian Current System: The Tasman Front, the East Auckland Current, and the East Cape Current, JPO 31, 2917–2943.","composite_is_model_output":false,"composite_is_simultaneous_geometry":false,"described_departure_lon_lat":[153,-33],"described_meander_latitude_band_south":[32,36],"figure_digitized":false,"latitude_band_is_current_width":false,"locator":"Introduction, p. 2918; Figure 2 caption p. 2919.","longitude_coverage_gap_east":[164,166],"original_source_sets_directly_inspected":false,"source_sets":[{"calendar_span_agrees_with_reported_duration":false,"duration_resolution":"Unresolved printed duration/date-span discrepancy; do not reinterpret 29 months as continuous coverage.","longitude_support_east":[153,164],"method":"Satellite infrared images","original_source":"Mulhearn (1987), as described by Tilburg et al. (2001)","source_reported_duration_months":29,"source_reported_period":"March 1982 to April 1985"},{"longitude_support_east":[166,170],"method":"Five cruises","original_source":"Stanton (1979), as described by Tilburg et al. (2001)","source_reported_period":"1966 to 1975"}],"source_url":"https://www.coaps.fsu.edu/bios/0110-2917.pdf"},"layer":"Historical infrared/front locations and climatological steric height relative to 2000 m are different diagnostic supports. Neither establishes a uniform-depth current axis or velocity-defined width.","name_equivalence_status":"Eastern extension of the EAC is a naming proposal in Oke et al. (2019); no automatic alias, new released identity or relation admitted.","observed_period":null,"remaining_gates":["Define the target as a historical composite, a dated jet, a mean-flow connection or a transport corridor before measuring it.","Inspect original satellite/cruise sources and resolve the printed 29-month/date-span discrepancy; retain unsynchronized coverage and the 164–166 E gap.","Use compatible velocity fields to diagnose any current axis; classify interrupted or multiple branches rather than forcing one connecting polyline.","Keep East Australian coastal reach and New Zealand boundary currents separate; review any naming equivalence explicitly.","Recover matching dated transverse boundaries and repeated comparable seasonal samples before width or annual ranges."],"route_construction_status":"reference_path_not_constructed_pending_geometry_definition","schema":"osw.current-source-scope-audit.v1","scope":"Historical composite frontal pathway compared with a reassessment of central Tasman Sea continuity. Named inventory entry retained; no single instantaneous axis or whole-current endpoints admitted.","source_access":"Oke et al. publisher-indexed abstract and Introduction and ANU repository abstract read. Direct publisher and author PDF retrieval failed; full 2019 article not inspected. Tilburg et al. author-hosted original PDF Introduction and Figure 2 caption read. No figure, velocity field or temperature section digitized.","source_citation":"Oke, Pilo, Ridgway, Kiss and Rykova (2019), A search for the Tasman Front, Journal of Marine Systems 199, 103217, doi:10.1016/j.jmarsys.2019.103217.","source_locator":"Publisher-indexed abstract and Introduction; author repository abstract. Historical comparison: Tilburg et al. (2001), Introduction, journal p. 2918, Figure 2 caption p. 2919.","source_url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0924796319300259","status":"editorial_source_scope_audit_not_canonical","time_convention":"Unsynchronized historical source sets and climatological interpretation remain separate from instantaneous and seasonal geometries.","whole_current_length_km":null,"whole_current_width_km":null},"note":{"audit_file":"research/tasman-front-composite-continuity-scope-audit.json","current_id":"tasman-front","label":"Historical composite and variable eastward pathways","observed_period":null,"related_current_ids":["east-australian"],"source_locator":"Publisher-indexed abstract and Introduction; author repository abstract. Historical comparison: Tilburg et al. (2001), Introduction, journal p. 2918, Figure 2 caption p. 2919.","source_url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0924796319300259","summary":"Historical composite paths combine different years and observation methods. A 2019 reassessment finds multiple pathways and questions a persistent narrow front across the central Tasman Sea. The 32–36 S meander band is not current width. A single dated axis, whole-current length and annual ranges remain unresolved."},"source_sha256":"87d760963c9b6465312010ac60214d9808f9dfcceef485c30174595e354c5677"}],"source_catalog_file":"research/ocean-current-reference-path-candidates.json","source_catalog_sha256":"b06caed27655c39f8127ddda441cba0a856800f599ec52edcd5b30d2ad37b633","source_decision":{"candidate_ids":["tasman-historical-connection-reach-reference-path-candidate"],"component_inventory_status":"not_assessed_by_this_planning_crosswalk","current_id":"tasman-front","existing_length_evidence_status":"no_numeric_length","existing_scope":"The East Australian Current separates eastward as the Tasman Front at a variable latitude, reported between 28°S and 37°S. The cited study does not give stable whole-front endpoints or an along-flow length; do not add this branch to the East Australian Current's coastal floor. A 2019 review found the front rarely distinct in instantaneous fields; a single stable path length would overstate that evidence.","known_component_currents":[],"ledger_kind":"eastward frontal current downstream of the East Australian Current","name":"Tasman Front","name_source_url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/2017JC013097","next_action":"Review the existing editorial route against scientific gates; broader/system/family scope remains governed by the planning strategy.","planning_basis":"The East Australian Current separates eastward as the Tasman Front at a variable latitude, reported between 28°S and 37°S. The cited study does not give stable whole-front endpoints or an along-flow length; do not add this branch to the East Australian Current's coastal floor. A 2019 review found the front rarely distinct in instantaneous fields; a single stable path length would overstate that evidence.","planning_status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","published_length_rank_eligible":false,"reference_path_decision":"editorial_candidate_present_scientific_review_pending","route_strategy_id":"dated_geometry","route_strategy_label":"Construct dated or regime-specific geometry","strategy_next_action":"Use dated or explicitly averaged geometry, distinguish an open loop from a closed circuit, and compare repeated regimes before summarizing."},"source_url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/2017JC013097","status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","strategy_id":"dated_geometry","strategy_label":"Construct dated or regime-specific geometry"},{"candidate_count":1,"construction_status":"editorial_candidate_present_scientific_review_pending","current_id":"tsushima","entity_id":"current:tsushima","id":"route-decision:tsushima","label":"Tsushima Current","next_action":"Review the existing editorial route against scientific gates; broader/system/family scope remains governed by the planning strategy.","rank_eligible":false,"route_ids":["tsushima-coastal-reference-path-candidate"],"scope":"A segment of the Kuroshio system in NOAA's glossary.","scope_reviews":[],"source_catalog_file":"research/ocean-current-reference-path-candidates.json","source_catalog_sha256":"b06caed27655c39f8127ddda441cba0a856800f599ec52edcd5b30d2ad37b633","source_decision":{"candidate_ids":["tsushima-coastal-reference-path-candidate"],"component_inventory_status":"not_assessed_by_this_planning_crosswalk","current_id":"tsushima","existing_length_evidence_status":"no_numeric_length","existing_scope":"A segment of the Kuroshio system in NOAA's glossary.","known_component_currents":[],"ledger_kind":"regional current","name":"Tsushima Current","name_source_url":"https://www.tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/glossary.html","next_action":"Review the existing editorial route against scientific gates; broader/system/family scope remains governed by the planning strategy.","planning_basis":"A segment of the Kuroshio system in NOAA's glossary.","planning_status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","published_length_rank_eligible":false,"reference_path_decision":"editorial_candidate_present_scientific_review_pending","route_strategy_id":"source_gated_regional_route","route_strategy_label":"Establish a sourced regional route","strategy_next_action":"Read primary geographic definitions, choose explicit endpoint/branch/layer/time conventions, and measure a reviewed route with alternatives; naming points alone are insufficient."},"source_url":"https://www.tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/glossary.html","status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","strategy_id":"source_gated_regional_route","strategy_label":"Establish a sourced regional route"},{"candidate_count":0,"construction_status":"reference_path_not_constructed","current_id":"west-australian","entity_id":"current:west-australian","id":"route-decision:west-australian","label":"West Australian Current","next_action":"Resolve the 1977 cyclonic-stream naming versus modern offshore northward flow using research/west-australian-current-naming-seasonal-scope-audit.json; its 800 km trough and 100-200 km width are unadmitted source context. Select layer/time-supported offshore velocity geometry and justify interface gates; coastal currents, particle release boundaries and inherited seasonal atlas patterns do not supply an axis.","rank_eligible":false,"route_ids":[],"scope":"No comparable path-length estimate admitted yet.","scope_reviews":[{"document":{"annual_length_range_km":null,"annual_width_range_km":null,"coastal_study":{"access":"full_page_read_2026-10-03","citation":"Ridgway and Condie (2004), doi:10.1029/2003JC001921","interpretation":"Coastal shelf-edge system and its regional names; its 5500 km winter system length is not transferable to offshore West Australian Current.","locator":"Abstract; Introduction and Study Domain","url":"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1029/2003JC001921"},"current_id":"west-australian","date":"2026-10-03","distinct_current_id":"leeuwin","historical_naming_lead":{"access":"publisher_indexed_abstract_read_2026-10-03; direct publisher full page unavailable; full paper not inspected","citation":"Andrews (1977), Eddy structure and the West Australian current, Deep Sea Research 24(12), 1133-1148, doi:10.1016/0146-6291(77)90517-3.","interpretation":"Abstract describes a cyclonic stream extending northeast from the Naturaliste Plateau and turning poleward at the coast. Its use of West Australian Current differs from the modern offshore northward definition; equivalence remains unreviewed.","locator":"Publisher abstract","reported_context":{"geometry_digitized":false,"measurement_admission":"unadmitted_historical_source_context","modern_offshore_current_identity_equivalence_established":false,"reported_scale_depth_m":370,"reported_stream_width_km":[100,200],"reported_trough_extent_km":800,"sampling":"Four cruises in the summers of 1972 and 1973; individual cruise dates unavailable in inspected abstract.","scale_depth_is_fixed_axis_layer":false,"study_area_degrees":[8,8],"trough_extent_is_modern_whole_current_length":false,"width_limits_are_seasonal_extrema":false},"url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0146629177905173"},"modern_pathway_context":{"access":"Full publisher page retrieved; Introduction and sections 2.1-2.4 read on 2026-10-03; no figures digitized or model data acquired.","citation":"Yit Sen Bull and van Sebille (2016), Sources, fate, and pathways of Leeuwin Current water in the Indian Ocean and Great Australian Bight: A Lagrangian study in an eddy-resolving ocean model, doi:10.1002/2015JC011486.","interpretation":"Leeuwin, its deeper equatorward undercurrent, and inshore summer Capes/Ningaloo flows are separately named. Subtropical onshore supply and particle connectivity do not diagnose a whole West Australian Current axis.","leeuwin_dimensions_transfer_to_west_australian":false,"locator":"Introduction; 2.2 Eulerian TROPAC01 Validation at 32 S; 2.3 Lagrangian Particle Model; 2.4 coastal sectors.","model_release_sections_are_current_endpoints":false,"model_support":"TROPAC01 1/10-degree model; 1960-2009 hindcast at five-day temporal means. This is model support, not West Australian Current observation dates.","model_validation_transport_agreement_establishes_width":false,"url":"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/2015JC011486","validation_caution":"Section 2.2 reports lower modeled flow speeds compensated by broader flow in transport validation. Agreement of integrated transport does not independently validate width or a centerline."},"name":"West Australian Current","observed_period":null,"primary_definition":{"access":"full_page_read_2026-10-03","interpretation":"Broad offshore northward subtropical-gyre flow; distinct from coastal southward Leeuwin. Definition supplies no diagnosed axis, numeric endpoint coordinates or physical width.","locator":"Definition paragraphs","url":"https://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/west-australian-current/"},"rejected_inferences":["No reuse of Leeuwin or 5500 km coastal-system path length.","No conversion of broad eastern Indian Ocean description into physical width or a representative single core.","No use of seasonal 20 S branching context as a universal whole-current endpoint.","No figure tracing or seasonal interpolation from inherited atlas patterns.","No transfer of the 1977 reported 800 km trough extent or 100-200 km cyclonic stream width into modern offshore-current dimensions before identity equivalence and full source methodology are reviewed.","The historical width interval is not a seasonal minimum/maximum range; a 370 m scale depth is not a uniform axis layer.","No substitution of coastal summer Capes/Ningaloo flow or deeper Leeuwin Undercurrent for the modern offshore West Australian Current.","Model particle release boundaries, sector limits and transport agreement are not current endpoint, width-edge or centerline diagnoses."],"remaining_gates":["Retrieve the full 1977 paper and resolve its poleward cyclonic-stream naming against the modern offshore northward flow; keep its reported dimensions in unadmitted source context meanwhile.","Choose representative layer and time support for a broad flow; consider a diagnostic flow corridor before a centerline.","Retrieve appropriate offshore velocity data and justify northern/southern interface gates independently of coastal-current geometry.","Read original seasonal atlas/data methodology before generating dated or seasonal routes.","Keep physical width, annual extrema and canonical measurement admission unresolved."],"schema":"osw.current-naming-scope-audit.v1","seasonal_context_lead":{"access":"full_pdf_read_2026-10-03","citation":"D’Adamo and Onton (2003), MMS-75/2003, dated 15 July 2003","interpretation":"Seasonal branching descriptions are inherited context, not newly acquired velocity geometry. Figure legend includes probable directions/speeds with insufficient data. Original atlas methodology and applicable samples require review.","locator":"Section 4 and Figure 12 caption, printed pp. 5 and 21 / PDF pp. 8 and 24","original_atlas_read":false,"provenance_chain":["D’Adamo and Onton (2003)","Buchan and Stroud (1993)","US Navy (1976) Climatic Atlas of the World"],"source_class":"government_synthesis_with_inherited_atlas_patterns","url":"https://library.dbca.wa.gov.au/static/FullTextFiles/065400.pdf"},"seasonal_route_playback_supported":false,"status":"editorial_scope_audit_not_scientific_approval","whole_current_length_km":null,"whole_current_width_km":null},"note":{"audit_file":"research/west-australian-current-naming-seasonal-scope-audit.json","current_id":"west-australian","label":"Offshore West Australian versus coastal Leeuwin flow","observed_period":null,"related_current_ids":["leeuwin"],"source_locator":"West Australian Current definition paragraphs","source_url":"https://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/west-australian-current/","summary":"Modern West Australian Current means broad northward offshore flow, distinct from coastal southward Leeuwin. Andrews (1977) used the name for a cyclonic stream turning poleward: its reported 800 km trough extent and 100-200 km stream width remain historical source context, not admitted dimensions for this current or seasonal extrema. Whole-current axis, endpoints, width and annual ranges remain unresolved.","supporting_sources":[{"access":"Indexed abstract inspected; full paper unavailable and not reviewed.","label":"Historical naming and dimensions: Andrews (1977)","locator":"Publisher abstract","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0146629177905173"},{"access":"Relevant full-text sections inspected; no model data or geometry extracted.","label":"Separate coastal flows and model limits: Yit Sen Bull and van Sebille (2016)","locator":"Introduction and sections 2.1-2.4","url":"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/2015JC011486"}]},"source_sha256":"55f4f9327a5211758bbaf422ff672a0fb39b63930c080df11677148d50cb5cdf"}],"source_catalog_file":"research/ocean-current-reference-path-candidates.json","source_catalog_sha256":"b06caed27655c39f8127ddda441cba0a856800f599ec52edcd5b30d2ad37b633","source_decision":{"candidate_ids":[],"component_inventory_status":"not_assessed_by_this_planning_crosswalk","current_id":"west-australian","existing_length_evidence_status":"no_numeric_length","existing_scope":"No comparable path-length estimate admitted yet.","known_component_currents":[],"ledger_kind":"eastern boundary current","name":"West Australian Current","name_source_url":"https://www.tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/glossary.html","next_action":"Resolve the 1977 cyclonic-stream naming versus modern offshore northward flow using research/west-australian-current-naming-seasonal-scope-audit.json; its 800 km trough and 100-200 km width are unadmitted source context. Select layer/time-supported offshore velocity geometry and justify interface gates; coastal currents, particle release boundaries and inherited seasonal atlas patterns do not supply an axis.","planning_basis":"No comparable path-length estimate admitted yet.","planning_status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","published_length_rank_eligible":false,"reference_path_decision":"reference_path_not_constructed","route_strategy_id":"resolve_naming_scope","route_strategy_label":"Resolve naming or extent convention","strategy_next_action":"Read competing definitions, resolve aliases versus distinct flows, and retain source-convention routes without merging their extents."},"source_url":"https://www.tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/glossary.html","status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","strategy_id":"resolve_naming_scope","strategy_label":"Resolve